# The Euclidean norm is continuous at the origin although it is not differentiable there.
# Before: the continuity strategy requested an axis-one derivative and rejected this norm.
# have fn norm(p finite_seq(R,2)) R = sqrt(p(1)^2+p(2)^2)
# $is_multi_continuous_at(norm,(0,0),2)
# Now: joint continuity follows coordinates, sums, powers and square-root continuity.
have fn norm(p finite_seq(R,2)) R = sqrt(p(1)^2+p(2)^2)
$is_multi_continuous_at(norm,(0,0),2)
$has_multi_limit_at(norm,(0,0),2,0)

# A fixed coordinate remains continuous and differentiable without deciding k = 1.
have k closed_range(1,2)
have fn projection(p finite_seq(R,2)) R = p(k)
$is_multi_continuous_at(projection,(0,0),2)
$is_multi_differentiable_at(projection,(0,0),2)
$has_multi_limit_at(projection,(0,0),2,(0,0)(k))
# Negative tests reject norm's derivative at the origin and a limit value of 1.
# Lean replay of the new joint continuity certificate remains explicitly unsupported.
# Evidence: target/release/litex -lang en -strict -f examples/proof_nodes/multivariable_continuity_without_derivative.lit
